dict(pas="8.11", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^如/if | 有/(one) had | 周公=zhōu gōng/the Duke of Zhou | 之/'s | 才=cái/talents | 之/(the) | 美，/fineness of | ^使/supposing | 驕/(he were) arrogant | 且/and | 吝，=lìn/mean, grudging | ^其/the | 餘/rest | 不/would not | 足/be worth | 觀/looking at | 也/(assertion) | 已。」/(and that is all)",
  l="The Master said: “If one had talents as fine as the Duke of Zhou's,\nsupposing he were arrogant and mean,\nthe rest would not be worth looking at.”",
  i="The Master said: “Give a man gifts as splendid as the Duke of Zhou's: if he is proud and tight-fisted, nothing else about him deserves a second look.”",
  n="如 'if' opens the supposition and 使 'supposing, should it be that' repeats it before the fault: two conditions, then the verdict. 周公之才之美 is 'the fineness of the Duke of Zhou's talents' — the second 之 links 才 to 美, 'the fineness of'. 其餘 'the rest of him'; 不足觀 'not enough to be looked at'; 也已 'and that is all', closing the judgement. Zhu Xi: 才美 is the fineness of intelligence, ability and skill; 驕 is boastful pride (矜夸), 吝 meanness and grudging (鄙嗇). He quotes Master Cheng: this puts in the strongest terms how impossible pride and meanness are — one with the Duke of Zhou's virtue would have neither of himself, but one with only his talents, if proud and mean, is not worth a look; and again: pride is the qi overflowing, meanness the qi shrinking. Zhu Xi adds his own view: though they differ as overflowing and shrinking, the two always go together — pride is the branches and leaves of meanness, meanness the root of pride; he had tested it on everyone, and never found a man proud and not mean, or mean and not proud. He Yan's Kong Anguo only identifies the Duke of Zhou as Dan 旦. Variant: He Yan's text ends 不足觀也已矣, with a further particle."),
