# ---------------- 8.15 ----------------
dict(pas="8.15", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^師摯=shī zhì/Music Master Zhi — the Grand Music-Master of Lu//master (of music)//Zhi (his personal name) | 之/'s | 始，《/beginning | 關雎》=guān jū/the Guanju, the first of the Odes | 之/'s | 亂，=luàn/finale | ^洋洋=yáng yáng/flowing full, rich and abundant//broad-flowing//broad-flowing | 乎/(how) | 盈/(it) filled | 耳/the ears | 哉！」/(exclamation)",
  l="The Master said: “At the start of Music Master Zhi, the finale of the Guanju —\nflowing full, how it filled the ears!”",
  i="The Master said: “When Music Master Zhi first took up his post, the closing strains of the Guanju came in a flood that left the hearing brimming over!”",
  n="Two phrases with 之 'of' set the scene; then the exclamation 洋洋乎…哉. 師 is the title 'music master', 摯 zhì his name (Zhu Xi: 摯音志): Zhu Xi, the music master of Lu named Zhi. 亂 here is not 'disorder' but the closing section of a piece of music (樂之卒章); Zhu Xi cites the Shiji: 'the finale of the Guanju was made the beginning of the Airs'. 洋洋 'broad-flowing', for a sense of beauty and fullness (美盛意); doubling makes it describe a manner. Zhu Xi reads 師摯之始 as a time: when Confucius came back to Lu from Wei and set the music right, it happened to be early in Master Zhi's tenure, so the music was as fine as this. He Yan's Zheng Xuan reads it otherwise: 始 is 首 'first', and 亂 is real disorder — when the Zhou Way declined, the tunes of Zheng and Wei arose and the correct music lost its measure; Zhi, the Grand Music-Master (大師) of Lu, knew the sound of the Guanju and was the first to set its disorder right. 關雎 is the Odes' first poem (3.20)."),
# ---------------- 8.16 ----------------
dict(pas="8.16", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^狂/wild, high-aiming | 而/yet | 不/not | 直，/straight | ^侗=tóng/ignorant, unformed | 而/yet | 不/not | 愿，=yuàn/careful and honest | ^悾悾=kōng kōng/witless, incapable//empty, simple//empty, simple | 而/yet | 不/without | 信，/good faith | ^吾/I | 不/do not | 知=zhī/know (what to make of) | 之/them | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “Wild, yet not straight;\nignorant, yet not careful;\nwitless, yet without good faith —\nI do not know what to make of them.”",
  i="The Master said: “Reckless but not honest, ignorant but not careful, simple-minded but not to be trusted — with men like that I can do nothing.”",
  n="Three lines of one shape, A 而不 B, 'A yet not B': each fault lacks the saving quality that usually goes with it. 狂 'wild, high-aiming' (5.21). 侗 'ignorant, unformed' (Zhu Xi: 無知貌); he reads it 音通, tōng, but modern practice reads this word tóng, which we give. 愿 yuàn 'careful and honest' (Zhu Xi: 謹厚也). 悾悾 kōng kōng (Zhu Xi: 音空) 'incapable-looking' (無能貌). 吾不知之矣 'I do not know them': Zhu Xi calls it a phrase of utter rejection — he would not even stoop to teach them. He quotes Su Shi: Heaven makes things with uneven endowments; below the middle grade, where there is a fault there is a matching virtue — the horse that kicks and bites is sure to run well, the one that cannot run is sure to be docile; to have the fault without the virtue makes one the world's waste material. He Yan's commentators: Kong Anguo, the wild should at least be straight, and 侗 is one not yet formed into a vessel, who ought at least to be careful; Bao Xian takes 悾悾 as 慤慤 'simple, plain', who ought at least to be trustworthy; Kong Anguo, all of these go against the normal measure, so 'I do not understand them'."),
