dict(pas="8.17", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^學/learn | 如/as if | 不/not | 及，/catching up | ^猶/still | 恐/fearing | 失/to lose | 之。」/it",
  l="The Master said: “Learn as if you were not catching up,\nand still fear to lose it.”",
  i="The Master said: “Study as one who runs after something always just out of reach — and even then be afraid that it will slip away.”",
  n="學如不及 'learn as though not reaching'; 猶 'still, even so'; 之 'it', what one is learning. Zhu Xi: when a man learns, he is already as if he could not reach it, and yet his heart still trembles lest he lose it — a warning that this is how a learner should be. He quotes Master Cheng: no letting anything slip past; the moment you say 'let it wait till tomorrow', it will not do. He Yan's commentary: learning comes in from outside and lasts only when it is ripe; so one is as if not reaching it, and still afraid of losing it."),
